      "faqs": [
        {
          "question": "Can my loved one keep their house if it's worth more than $5,000?",
          "answer": "Yes. The primary home where your loved one lives is completely exempt from SNAP asset limits, regardless of its value. In Texas the countable asset limit is $5,000 for all households. Texas primarily counts liquid assets like bank accounts and stocks toward this limit."
        },
        {
          "question": "Does Social Security income disqualify my loved one from SNAP?",
          "answer": "Not necessarily. Social Security counts as income, but seniors get to deduct medical expenses over $35/month and high shelter costs first. If your loved one has significant medical bills or housing costs, their net income may fall below the $1,330/month limit even with Social Security."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can I apply for SNAP on behalf of my elderly loved one?",
          "answer": "Yes, family members can apply as an authorized representative if your loved one cannot apply themselves due to age, disability, or other barriers. You'll need to complete additional paperwork during the application process to establish this relationship."
        },
        {
          "question": "What happens if my loved one's income changes after approval?",
          "answer": "Report income changes to HHSC within 10 days by calling (877) 541-7905 or logging into YourTexasBenefits.com. Benefits are recertified every 36 months for seniors, and HHSC will adjust the benefit amount based on new income information."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can my loved one use SNAP benefits to buy groceries online?",
          "answer": "Yes, Texas participates in SNAP online purchasing. Your loved one can use their EBT card to buy eligible groceries for delivery or pickup from participating retailers like Amazon, Walmart, and H-E-B through their websites or apps. Important for 2026: as of April 1, 2026, Texas law prohibits using SNAP benefits to buy candy or sweetened drinks (like soda), whether shopping online or in-person."
        },
        {
          "question": "What if my loved one owns a car worth more than $5,000?",
          "answer": "One vehicle per household is exempt regardless of value if used for transportation. Additional vehicles may count toward the asset limit, but Texas has exceptions for work vehicles, vehicles needed for medical transportation, and vehicles being used to produce income."
        }
      ],
