      "contentSections": [],
      "faqs": [
        {
          "question": "What if my loved one's mobile home isn't eligible in their region?",
          "answer": "Mobile home eligibility varies by regional agency. Some exclude them entirely (like ATCog), while others include them. Call your regional subrecipient directly to confirm whether mobile homes qualify in your area, as this rule isn't consistent statewide."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can I apply on behalf of my elderly loved one?",
          "answer": "Yes, family members can help with the application process. However, your loved one will need to sign forms and provide their personal documentation. Some agencies may require your loved one to be present for the initial energy audit scheduling."
        },
        {
          "question": "How long is the waitlist really?",
          "answer": "Waitlists vary dramatically by region and funding cycles. Some areas process applications within months, while others have year-long waits. However, households with elderly members, people with disabilities, or children under 6 get priority points that can significantly reduce wait times."
        },
        {
          "question": "What if the landlord won't sign the permission form?",
          "answer": "Unfortunately, weatherization services cannot proceed without a signed Landlord Permission Form. The program requires access to the property for an audit and contractor work. If the landlord refuses, your loved one may still qualify for utility bill assistance through the CEAP program, which often does not require physical home modifications."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can my loved one apply if they received weatherization services in the past?",
          "answer": "In most Texas regions, a property is ineligible if it has been weatherized by WAP within the last 15 years. If the previous service was more than 15 years ago, they may be eligible to apply again for updated energy-efficiency measures."
        },
        {
          "question": "What happens if the energy audit shows my loved one's home doesn't need weatherization?",
          "answer": "No services will be provided if the audit determines the home doesn't meet energy-savings requirements. The audit itself is free, but weatherization work only proceeds if it meets Department of Energy efficiency standards for cost-effectiveness."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can my loved one apply for both weatherization and LIHEAP at the same time?",
          "answer": "Yes, these are separate programs and you can apply for both simultaneously. In fact, some regional agencies coordinate these services or may suggest applying for LIHEAP while on the weatherization waitlist for immediate utility bill help."
        },
        {
          "question": "What if my loved one's income includes irregular Social Security or pension payments?",
          "answer": "You must provide proof of all gross income received in the 30 days prior to the application date. If a regular payment was missed or an extra one was received in that window, provide the official Award Letter from the Social Security Administration or pension source to help the agency accurately annualize the income."
        }
      ],
