      "faqs": [
        {
          "question": "Can my loved one keep their house if it's worth more than the asset limit?",
          "answer": "Yes, your loved one's primary home is exempt from the $2,000 asset limit as long as they live there or intend to return, and the home equity doesn't exceed $752,000 (2026). The home remains exempt even if your spouse or disabled child lives there."
        },
        {
          "question": "How long is the waitlist really?",
          "answer": "Wait times vary from several months to multiple years depending on your region and your loved one's priority level. Urban areas like Houston and Dallas typically have longer waits than rural areas. Those at highest risk of immediate nursing home placement get priority."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can I apply on behalf of my elderly loved one?",
          "answer": "Yes, you can apply as your loved one's authorized representative if they cannot apply themselves due to physical or cognitive limitations. You'll need to complete additional paperwork establishing your authority to act on their behalf."
        },
        {
          "question": "What if my loved one's income is slightly over the $2,829 limit?",
          "answer": "Your loved one may still qualify through a Medicaid spend-down process, where they pay the excess income toward medical expenses each month. Contact HHSC at (877) 541-7905 to discuss spend-down options for your loved one's specific situation."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can my loved one receive CBA waiver services and still live in assisted living?",
          "answer": "Yes, CBA waiver services can be provided in various community settings including assisted living facilities, as long as your loved one isn't in a nursing facility. The services complement rather than replace the assisted living care."
        },
        {
          "question": "What happens if my loved one's needs increase after they're enrolled?",
          "answer": "The care coordinator will reassess your loved one's needs and can modify the service plan accordingly, as long as the total cost doesn't exceed the nursing facility payment rate. Regular reassessments ensure services match current needs."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can my loved one choose their own personal care attendant?",
          "answer": "In many cases, yes. Your loved one may be able to hire family members or friends as personal attendants, though specific rules vary by the managed care organization. The attendant must meet training and background check requirements."
        },
        {
          "question": "What if we move to a different part of Texas after enrollment?",
          "answer": "Your loved one can maintain CBA waiver services when moving within Texas, but you'll need to transfer to the new region's STAR+PLUS managed care organization. Contact your current care coordinator to initiate the transfer process before moving."
        }
      ],
