      "intro": "If your parent struggles with bathing, dressing, getting to the toilet, eating, or managing meals on their own, Community Attendant Services (CAS) can send a paid in-home attendant to help, at no cost to your family. The program is for Texans of any age who have a documented medical reason for needing that help but who do not qualify for standard Medicaid.\n\nCAS is funded through Texas Medicaid's state plan and is not a capped waiver with a waitlist. If your parent meets the financial and functional requirements, they are entitled to services. The financial limit is $2,982/month in income for a single applicant (2026), with a resource cap of $2,000 in countable assets. The functional bar is a score of at least 24 on a state assessment form (Form 2060), which a regional nurse conducts in person.\n\nThis is not a simple program to navigate. It requires a practitioner's statement of medical need, a financial determination through the state's MEPD process, and a functional assessment before a nurse can authorize services. The steps below walk your family through exactly what to expect and what to gather before calling.",
